UPDATE 2026-09-06 (seat06): ROOT-CAUSED via gdb (breakpoint on core_runtime_error/core_icn_error,
backtraces captured), routed to hq_B -- TWO INDEPENDENT, UNRELATED DEFECTS land on this one test, which
is why the raw diff looked like a single confusing "control-flow/count divergence": (1) `errorclear()`
(source line 20) has NO builtin dispatch anywhere in SCRIP -- it falls through to the undefined-function
path and would ABORT the program right there if &error-conversion weren't separately (accidentally)
converting that specific abort into a failure; see icon-errorclear-builtin-is-entirely-unimplemented-
and-aborts-the-program (rank 2, hq_B). (2) `&null()` (source line 11, calling a non-procedure value)
NEVER raises Icon's error 106 ("procedure or integer expected") at all -- it just silently fails with
zero &error/&errornumber/&errortext/&errorvalue bookkeeping, a general gap in `rt_call_value()`
unrelated to (1); see icon-call-of-a-non-callable-value-never-raises-error-106-or-updates-error-state
(rank 2, hq_B). What LOOKS like the real error's info arriving one write-group late is actually
`errorclear()`'s own accidental error (code 22, "Undefined function called", value always `&null`)
landing where a human expects `&null()`'s error (106) to have persisted -- there was never anything
real there to persist, because of defect (2). This marker stays until both rows land AND the full
program is re-diffed against icont end-to-end (this row's own DONE-WHEN only proves the two isolated
minimal witnesses, not the combined 18-line output).
